+\name{CheckATACData}
+\alias{CheckATACData}
+\title{Check an ATAC object without changing it}
+\usage{
+CheckATACData(object, assay = NULL, genome = NULL, sample.by = NULL)
+}
+\arguments{
+\item{object}{A Seurat object with a Signac \code{ChromatinAssay}.}
+
+\item{assay}{Assay to check; defaults to the object's default assay.}
+
+\item{genome}{Optional expected genome label. Unknown references are
+reported, never inferred from chromosome spelling.}
+
+\item{sample.by}{Optional metadata column containing sample IDs. This checks
+complete labels, not whether samples are independent biological replicates.}
+}
+\value{
+A list with \code{valid}, \code{errors}, \code{warnings}, \code{assay}, \code{n_cells},
+\code{n_peaks}, \code{genome}, \code{samples}, a \code{fragments} resource table, and
+\code{fragments_ready}. \code{valid} describes the matrix contract. Fragment readiness
+checks local file/index existence and cell mapping for resources with cells
+in the selected assay. The resource table retains all attached files; its
+\code{active} column identifies those required for this assay. Empty mappings
+left by native subsetting do not block readiness. It does not hash files,
+scan their contents, or guarantee fragment-dependent computation will pass.
+Use Signac's \code{ValidateFragments()} for content validation when required.
+}
+\description{
+Report raw-count, peak-coordinate, cell/metadata and reference alignment for
+an existing Seurat \code{ChromatinAssay}. Fragment path/index availability is
+reported separately, so an otherwise valid matrix can be analysed without
+fragments. This check does not install packages, retrieve resources, modify
+the object, or inspect dependencies of downstream optional analyses.
+}
+\examples{
+counts <- Matrix::sparseMatrix(
+  i = c(1, 2), j = c(1, 2), x = c(2, 3), dims = c(2, 2),
+  dimnames = list(c("p1", "p2"), c("cell1", "cell2"))
+)
+ranges <- GenomicRanges::GRanges(c("chr1:1-50", "chr1:101-150"))
+object <- ReadATACData(counts, ranges = ranges, genome = "hg38")
+report <- CheckATACData(object)
+report$valid
+report$fragments_ready
+}
+\seealso{
+\code{\link[=ReadATACData]{ReadATACData()}}, \code{\link[=RunStandardWorkflow]{RunStandardWorkflow()}}
+}
